  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n\nWe will employ a multi-resolution optimization approach to tackle the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The core idea is to:\n\n   - Use several different grid resolutions (n=100, 200, 400, 700) to transfer solutions between scales\n   - Apply coordinate descent with random initialization for each resolution\n   - Combine local optimization (gradient descent) with stochastic search components\n   - Add a smoothing term to the objective function to escape local minima\n\nThis differs significantly from the submitted program's methods by using:\n\n   - Explicit control over grid resolutions instead of adaptive scale searches\n   - Multiple fixed resolution optimizations rather than hierarchical parent-child transfers\n   - Stochastic search components to guide optimization\n   \ufe0f These changes should produce better global solutions by preventing premature convergence to high-overlap states.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n\nVariables:  \n   - Piecewise constant function h: [0,2] -> [0,1]\n   - Sampled at n points with spacing dx=2/n\n   - Integral constraint: \u222bh(x)dx = 1 \u2192 sum(h) = n/2\n\nObjective:  \n   C\u2085 = (2/n) * max_{k} [ np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')[k] ]\n\nSmoothed Objective:  \n   E(h) = C\u2085 + \u03bb * (ISTA-style regularization term) + penalized integration term\n\nConstraints:  \n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - sum(h) = n/2\n\nRepresentation:  \n   h represents a piecewise constant function over [0,2] at n points\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n\nFor run(seed, budget_s, **kwargs):\n   - Initialize best_c5 = \u221e, best_h = None\n   - Base grid: n_sequence = [100,200,400,700] (run in order)\n   - For n in n_sequence:\n        a) Generate candidate h_init at this resolution if not using parent\n        b) Clip and project to [0,1] with integral 1\n        c) Perform coordinate descent with adaptive step size\n        d) Apply random logarithmic shifts and coordinate permutations\n        e) Run local optimization using SLSQP with projected gradients\n        f) Update best_c5/ best_h if improved\n   - Ensure solution is evaluated with exact provided correlator\n   - Return (best_h, best_c5, len(best_h))\n\nKey helper functions:\n   - transfer_parent(h, target_n): Interpolates/upsampling using \n        h_target = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2, target_n), np.linspace(0,2,len(h)), h)\n   - project_feasible(x): Adjusts integral and bounds while maintaining structure\n   \nImplementation details for coordinate descent at resolution n:\n   - Initialize h0 uniformly in [0,1] (or from parent if available)\n   - Set \u03bb = 0.1 * (n + \u2460/trials) for regularization\n   - Perform 200 iterations of coordinate descent with \n        {h[p] = clip(0,1, h[p] + g[p] * step)}\n   - Update step size based on directional derivative\n   - Hybridize with local SLSQP optimizer every 100 steps\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n\nResolutions to try: [100,200,400,700]\nSloppy_evaluation = flag to evaluate only key shifts for rapid screening\nDescent_modes = [dictionary_search, gradient_ascent, simulated_annealing]\nOptimization_schedulers = [constant_step, adaptive_step_with_backtracking, stochastic_step]\n\nApply these in a fixed, documented order:\n   Phase 1: Evaluate candidate at current resolution  \n   Phase 2: Run coordinate descent for baseline  \n   Phase 3: Apply single random component mutation  \n   Phase 4: Run local optimizer  \n   Phase 5: Transfer to next resolution if time allows  \n\n5. Budget and Robustness:\n\nTime budget tracking:\n   - For each resolution, allocate time proportional to max(n)  \n   - Upgrade parameters for larger n (more iterations)\n   - Flag time_per_point = budget_s / total_points\n   - Absolute cutoff = budget_s - 30 seconds\n\nFeasibility checks:\n   - Ensure integral clipping preserves sum(h) = n/2\n   - Check range 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 after all operations\n   - Maintain smoothness regularization parameter \u03bb\n\nFallback strategy if optimization fails:\n   - Use transfer_parent(parent, n) if parent exists\n   - Use random initialization if parent not available\n   - Default to provided initial_h_values if available\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n\nFinal solution must satisfy:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - Compute C\u2085 with np.correlate mode='full' and proper scaling\n   - Selection criteria: lower C\u2085 is always better \n   - For a resolution n, the solution h must have length n\n   - Leading comments in code provide method details for peer review\n\nPost-optimization checks:\n   - Validate against provided C\u2085 calculation method\n   - Double-check integral (sum(h) must be n/2)\n   - Ensure output follows specified format exactly\n\nFor a detailed implementation of the optimization steps and their failures, refer to the appended specification.",
